the vaccine program has exceeded  expectations but it is about to become  the victim of its own success the reason  is what we might call the second dose  debt now this chart shows where we are  and where the government wants to get to  as of yesterday almost 26 million people  had received a first dose that's the  blue lines  and the lower yellow line shows almost 2  million people have had a second dose  the target is for the 32 million people  in the top  nine priority groups to have had their  first dose by the 15th of april  but the real date that's been driving  the program is the 29th  of march that's 12 weeks from when the  rollout began in earnest when the  astrazeneca jab  joined the pfizer jab in circulation and  12 weeks  is when second doses become due as of  today the clock is ticking  for 23.9 million people in need of that  second jab  and that's why the delay to more than  five million doses couldn't have come  at a worse time just as the program  needs to accelerate  the nhs has had to effectively pause  first doses  in april now this chart shows how the  first  dose boom becomes a second dose  debt now the blue bars here are the  first doses that were delivered  since january now from april they become  second doses that need to be delivered  we've used the current seven day rolling  average of more than 450 000 doses to  show what it looks like which is why  the line flattens off you can see there  are no first doses  at all in april where matt hancock says  12 million  second shots will be prioritized but  even so based on  current supply the second doses have  caught up the debt  has been paid by the first week in june  now this does mean everyone under 50 is  going to have to wait a little longer  than they hope for a shirt first shot  but based on current supply the program  is still  on track here you can see our projection  based on  current supply levels and you see the  flattening off there  in april when there are no first doses  but on this model  every adult still gets a first shot by  july the 18th  that's nearly two weeks ahead of target  and look second doses  have reached 31 million that's almost  everyone in the most vulnerable groups  and that could be everyone having had a  second dose by mid-september  if this rate continues now these are  just  projections and it's clear this delay  has cost the government some of its  headroom  and further hitches will have  consequences  but for now the vaccine program is still  on course for a remarkable success
 

